    "lawCode": "Amd §1020-f, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6426\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the results\nof an annual stress test of all systems that support outage management\nand communications of the Long Island power authority\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill requires service providers of the Long Island Power Authority\nto successfully complete an annual stress test of all outage management\nand communications systems.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the public authorities law section 1020-f, subsection\n(cc), by adding a new subdivision 6. This new section will require that\nall service providers of the Long Island Power Authority successfully\ncomplete an annual stress test of all outage management and communi-\ncation systems. This section provides for a fine of $250,000 per day for\nfailure to successfully complete the annual stress test, prohibits the\nfine being used as a basis in any rate calculation and instead provides\nthat the fine will be disbursed on a prorated basis to all customers' in\nthe service territory.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nTropical Storm Isaias left too many Long Islanders in the dark for too\nlong. The failure of PSEG's outage management and communications systems\nlead to prolonged outages, confusion, and misuse of resources. The fail-\nure of these systems was avoidable. Proper oversight by the Long Island\nPower Authority would have revealed the failures of PSEG's system prior\nto the storm.\n\nAs a result, the Long Island Power Authority required that their service\nprovider undertake an immediate stress test of its systems.  Astounding-\nly, to date, the stress test has still not been successfully completed.\nThis leaves Long Islanders susceptible to another storm, as hurricane\nseason again approaches.\n\nThis bill will require that all contracts entered in to by LIPA with any\nservice provider contain a component requiring regular stress testing of\nall outage management and communications systems.  Penalties will be\nassessed in the event that the service providers fails to meet its\ncontractual duties, which will be disbursed to rate payers in the LIPA\nservice territory as a rebate.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately and will apply to contracts\nentered in to or amended on or after the effective date.\n",
