    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 81\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 6426-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  public authorities law, in relation to the\n      results of an annual stress  test  of  all  systems  that  support\n      outage  and  restoration management and communications of the Long\n      Island power authority\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill, introduced in the wake  of  Tropical  Storm  Isaias,  would\nrequire  a service provider of the Long Island Power Authority (LIPA) to\n\"successfully complete\" and submit to LIPA an \"annual  stress  test\"  of\nvarious  systems  that  \"support  outage  and restoration management and\ncommunications.\" LIPA would be required  to  make  the  results  of  any\nstress test available to the New York State Department of Public Service\n(DPS),  certify  to  DPS  the receipt of a successfully completed annual\nstress test, and publish the  results  of  the  stress  test  on  LIPA's\nwebsite within 72 hours of its receipt of stress test results.\n \n  The  bill  would  also require any \"management agreement\" between LIPA\nand the service provider to specify the dates on which the  stress  test\nwill be conducted and when the service provider must provide the results\nto  LIPA.  Finally,  the  bill  would mandate the imposition of a \"civil\npenalty\" on the service provider in the amount of $250,000 per day for a\nfailure to successfully complete the annual stress test.\n \n  I am advised that, as a result of  the  state's  post-Isaias  investi-\ngations, LIPA's service provider, PSEG Long Island LLC (PSEG), has filed\nan  amended  Emergency  Response  Plan  (ERP) which requires the service\nprovider to test its Outage Management System (OMS) more frequently then\nunder the prior ERP and using more stringent parameters, including test-\ning OMS with an outage impacting 90% or more of customers over a 24-hour\nperiod, to ensure the testing is consistent with realistic outage condi-\ntions and that the OMS will perform in a major outage event, as well  as\nprovisions for re-testing and corrective actions should stress tests not\nmeet  specified  standards.  The  service  provider  is also required to\nperform semi-annual capacity testing of other critical systems, such  as\ncall center and back-up call center functions. Detailed results of test-\ning by the service provider must be provided to DPS and LIPA.\n \n  In  addition,  shortly after this bill was passed, LIPA reached agree-\nment with PSEG on a new four-year  operations  services  agreement.  The\nreformed  agreement  was  approved  by the LIPA Board of Trustees at its\nmeeting on December 15, 2021. I am advised that the  reformed  agreement\nrepresents  a  comprehensive  approach  aimed at preventing the types of\nfailures that led to the performance issues surrounding  Tropical  Storm\nIsaias,  and  includes provisions for the imposition of financial penal-\nties for failure to comply with the updated ERP and  other  obligations,\nnew  commitments  to  address  known operational issues to ensure timely\ncorrective action, a duty  of  candor  requiring  timely  disclosure  of\nsignificant  operational issues that could impair the service provider's\nability to provide safe and reliable electric  service  and  meet  other\n \nobligations, and provisions for independent verification, validation and\ntesting  of  the  performance of mission-critical information technology\nsystems.\n \n  The  intent  of this bill is laudable, and I agree that adequate safe-\nguards must be in place to ensure that outage and restoration management\nand communications systems work effectively to enable LIPA customers  to\nreport outages and obtain critically important information about outages\nand  service restoration in a timely manner. However, the highly techni-\ncal requirements for these complex protocols are best addressed  through\nregulation,  not  legislation, particularly when it is unlikely that any\npiece of legislation would be able to articulate the specific  technical\nstandards  and  other  metrics  necessary  to  determine the efficacy of\npotential stress tests.\n \n  Given the revised agreement, which is subject to regulatory review  by\npublic officials, I am constrained to veto this duplicative legislation.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
